1
00:00:02,441 --> 00:00:04,989
This is your telescope.

2
00:00:05,282 --> 00:00:07,360
An engineering marvel.

3
00:00:07,513 --> 00:00:09,916
An exploration powerhouse.

4
00:00:10,259 --> 00:00:12,547
Use it to look back in time

5
00:00:12,588 --> 00:00:16,825
and explore the first galaxies
that formed after the Big Bang;

6
00:00:17,782 --> 00:00:21,556
to peer into atmospheres of planets
orbiting the stars.

7
00:00:21,837 --> 00:00:27,313
It’s your eye-piece to the uncharted,
unknown and unimagined.

8
00:00:31,502 --> 00:00:35,015
This is the largest, most complex

9
00:00:35,057 --> 00:00:38,833
and challenging space telescope
ever constructed.

10
00:00:38,888 --> 00:00:41,498
It will change our understanding
of the universe

11
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:43,493
and our place in it.

12
00:00:43,733 --> 00:00:46,297
The James Webb Space Telescope.

13
00:00:55,183 --> 00:00:59,230
Equipped with the largest primary mirror
ever to be flown in space;

14
00:00:59,277 --> 00:01:02,541
at 6.5 meters, it’s more than 6 times

15
00:01:02,581 --> 00:01:06,545
the size of the Hubble Space
Telescope primary mirror.

16
00:01:08,580 --> 00:01:12,265
Webb’s four, cutting-edge, 
infrared instruments and cameras

17
00:01:12,311 --> 00:01:15,528
operate at super cold temperatures;

18
00:01:15,926 --> 00:01:18,927
temperatures colder
than the surface of Pluto.

19
00:01:21,786 --> 00:01:26,759
Getting this cold is done with the help
of the largest sunshield ever flown;

20
00:01:26,791 --> 00:01:29,879
a 5-layer, tennis court-size sunshield

21
00:01:29,926 --> 00:01:33,554
that blocks heat from the Sun,
Earth and Moon.

22
00:01:38,719 --> 00:01:40,610
Webb will be the first telescope

23
00:01:40,656 --> 00:01:44,477
to detect light from the most distant
galaxies in the Universe.

24
00:01:45,142 --> 00:01:49,704
These first galaxies formed
about 13.5 billion years ago,

25
00:01:49,735 --> 00:01:53,545
only 300 or so million years
after the Big Bang.

26
00:01:54,249 --> 00:01:56,695
Webb carries advanced technologies

27
00:01:56,741 --> 00:02:01,040
to tackle some of the most fundamental
questions about the Universe.

28
00:02:01,308 --> 00:02:04,055
How did the first galaxies
form and evolve?

29
00:02:04,207 --> 00:02:07,204
Are there chemical signatures
of the building-blocks of life

30
00:02:07,244 --> 00:02:08,791
on other worlds?

31
00:02:09,094 --> 00:02:11,178
Is our solar system unique?

32
00:02:15,586 --> 00:02:20,766
Launching such a large telescope into space
is an incredible engineering challenge.

33
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:26,203
Fully deployed, Webb is too large
to fit inside any rocket fairing.

34
00:02:26,232 --> 00:02:30,866
Engineers designed it to be folded,
like origami, to squeeze inside

35
00:02:30,921 --> 00:02:35,955
the European Space Agency’s
5-meter diameter Ariane 5 rocket fairing.

36
00:02:39,281 --> 00:02:43,558
After launch, controllers on the ground
deploy Webb remotely.

37
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:47,143
Deployment is an intricate ballet.

38
00:02:47,495 --> 00:02:51,547
For nearly 3-weeks,
controllers carefully unfold Webb.

39
00:02:51,765 --> 00:02:55,990
After this delicate dance, Webb’s
golden mirrors are precisely aligned

40
00:02:56,030 --> 00:02:59,591
using motors behind
each hexagonal mirror segment,

41
00:02:59,643 --> 00:03:03,679
adjusting them to form
one, perfect mirror.

42
00:03:04,174 --> 00:03:08,514
Once the instruments are cooled,
the exploration will begin.

43
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:13,252
Webb is a technological challenge
like no other.

44
00:03:13,282 --> 00:03:15,769
Born of the efforts of thousands of people

45
00:03:15,791 --> 00:03:18,832
across the United States,
Canada and Europe.

46
00:03:19,709 --> 00:03:23,556
The James Webb Space Telescope
is your telescope.

47
00:03:23,628 --> 00:03:25,469
Use it to explore.

48
00:03:25,502 --> 00:03:27,281
To challenge theories.

49
00:03:27,311 --> 00:03:29,487
To see sights yet unseen.

50
00:03:29,688 --> 00:03:35,447
It’s yours to unfold the beauty
and mystery in the Universe, and…

51
00:03:35,501 --> 00:03:37,255
our place in it.

52
00:03:38,631 --> 00:03:41,352
[Explore Solar System &amp; Beyond]

53
00:03:41,931 --> 00:03:43,553
[NASA]